<header><p class="st-kicker">nomadic confederation · Central Asia and the Steppe</p><h1 class="st-title">Chionites</h1><p class="st-meta">350 CE – 440 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Hunnic invaders of Bactria in the 4th century who fought both against and alongside Sasanian Persia, including at the siege of Amida in 359.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Chionites, Xionites in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> and Xiyon in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-persian">Middle Persian</a>, appear in Roman and Persian sources from the mid-4th century as nomads who moved into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transoxiana">Transoxiana</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/greco-bactrian-kingdom">Bactria</a> from the north, threatening the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kushano-sasanian-kingdom">Kushano-Sasanian Kingdom</a>. Their exact origin and relation to the European <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a> are debated.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shapur-ii">Shapur II</a> fought them on his eastern frontier around 350 before making an alliance, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ammianus-marcellinus">Ammianus Marcellinus</a> describes their king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grumbates">Grumbates</a> leading Chionite troops alongside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> at the siege of the Roman city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amida">Amida</a> in 359, where Grumbates' son was killed. Little is known of their internal organisation beyond rule by kings over mounted warrior bands.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The Chionites appear to have given rise to, or been absorbed by, the later Hunnic dynasties of the region, notably the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kidarite-kingdom">Kidarite Kingdom</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/alchon-huns">Alchon Huns</a>, by the early 5th century.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/chionites.jpg" alt="Chionites" width="320" height="195"><figcaption>Image: Thomas A. Lessman, en:User:Talessman · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Asia_400ad.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>350 CE – 440 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>nomadic confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>nomadic</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Grumbates</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>unknown</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Uzbekistan, Afghanistan, Turkmenistan</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Xionites, Hunnoi of Bactria</dd></dl></aside></div>
